Lecture 1 — Why Load Balancer Exists
Most courses start with:
"A Load Balancer distributes requests."
That sentence is true, but it doesn't explain why the component exists.
Let's start from a system with exactly one server.
Users

   |
   |
Internet
   |
   |
+-------------+
| Java Server |
+-------------+
Suppose your Spring Boot application runs on one machine.
Configuration:
•	8 CPU cores
•	32 GB RAM
•	Tomcat
•	PostgreSQL
Everything works.
Now imagine your application becomes popular.
100 users
No problem.
1000 users
Still okay.
10,000 users
CPU reaches 100%.
Memory usage rises.
Thread pool becomes full.
Requests wait.
Latency increases.
Eventually:
Request Timeout
Then:
Connection Refused
Finally:
Server Crash
The question is:
Why not simply buy a larger server?
Suppose your current server has:
8 CPU
Upgrade to:
16 CPU
Later:
32 CPU
Then:
64 CPU
Eventually:
128 CPU
You discover a practical limit.
At some point:
•	CPUs stop scaling linearly.
•	Memory access becomes slower due to NUMA effects.
•	Hardware becomes disproportionately expensive.
•	The server is still a single point of failure.
This is called vertical scaling.
1 server

↓

More CPU

↓

More RAM

↓

Bigger server
It cannot continue indefinitely.
________________________________________
The Better Idea
Instead of one huge machine...
             ?
User -----> Server A

             ?
User -----> Server B

             ?
User -----> Server C
Now you have three machines.
Each handles roughly one-third of the traffic.
This is horizontal scaling.
But a new question appears:
How does the user know which server to contact?
The browser knows only one address:
www.mycompany.com
It doesn't know:
Server 1
Server 2
Server 3
If DNS randomly returns one IP, what happens if that server is down?
Users receive errors.
So we need one component that always accepts requests and decides where each request should go.
                 +------------------+
Users ---------->| Load Balancer    |
                 +------------------+

                   |      |      |
                   |      |      |
                Server1 Server2 Server3
This component becomes the traffic controller.
Its first responsibility is not "balancing"—it's routing every incoming request to a healthy backend.
________________________________________
Is It Really That Simple?
Suppose there are three servers:
Server A
Server B
Server C
Each can process:
100 requests/sec
A burst arrives:
300 requests
An ideal distribution is:
100 → A
100 → B
100 → C
Now suppose Server B becomes slower because it starts a long garbage collection pause.
A naive round-robin algorithm still sends every third request to B.
A → 10 ms
B → 2 s
C → 12 ms
Users routed to B experience terrible latency even though A and C are healthy.
A production-grade load balancer continuously evaluates backend health and, depending on the algorithm, may temporarily stop sending traffic to B until it recovers.
This raises deeper questions we'll answer next:
•	How does a load balancer know a server is unhealthy?
•	What is an active health check versus a passive one?
•	Why isn't round robin always the best algorithm?
•	What is Least Connections?
•	What is Least Response Time?
•	What happens when a backend dies in the middle of processing a request?
•	How do cloud load balancers handle millions of concurrent TCP connections without becoming bottlenecks themselves?
Those questions lead us into the internal architecture of modern load balancers.

