Load Balancer — Lesson 2: What Actually Happens to a Request?
Now we're going below HTTP.
Our goal is to understand what physically/logically happens between:
Client
   ↓
Load Balancer
   ↓
Java Application
We'll start with the simplest case:
Client ────────► L4 Load Balancer ────────► Java Server
No API Gateway. No service mesh. No Kafka. Just networking.
________________________________________
1. Start with the client
You write:
https://api.example.com/orders/123
Your application doesn't initially know:
10.10.2.15
It knows a hostname:
api.example.com
So the first thing that happens is DNS resolution.
Conceptually:
Client
   |
   | "What IP is api.example.com?"
   v
DNS
   |
   | 203.0.113.10
   v
Client
The returned IP might represent the load-balancing frontend, not your Java server.
So:
203.0.113.10
could be:
                 Public Internet
                       |
                       v
               203.0.113.10
                       |
                Load Balancer
                       |
              ┌────────┼────────┐
              ▼        ▼        ▼
            App A    App B    App C
This distinction is extremely important.
The public IP doesn't have to belong to your application.
________________________________________
2. Now TCP enters the picture
Suppose the client got:
203.0.113.10
and the service uses HTTPS:
port 443
The client needs a TCP connection.
The TCP handshake is approximately:
Client                         LB
  |                             |
  | -------- SYN -------------> |
  |                             |
  | <------- SYN-ACK ---------- |
  |                             |
  | -------- ACK -------------> |
  |                             |
  |       TCP established       |
Now we have:
Client
   │
   │ TCP connection
   ▼
Load Balancer
Notice something:
At this moment, we haven't sent the HTTP request yet.
TCP is underneath HTTP.
This distinction matters enormously.
________________________________________
3. Now HTTPS/TLS happens
After TCP is established, TLS negotiation happens.
Conceptually:
Client
   │
   │ TCP
   ▼
LB
   │
   │ TLS handshake
   ▼
LB
Eventually we have an encrypted channel.
Then:
GET /orders/123 HTTP/1.1
Host: api.example.com
travels inside the TLS connection.
Now here's the first major architectural fork.
________________________________________
4. Does the Load Balancer understand /orders/123?
Depends.
L4 Load Balancer
At Layer 4, it primarily works with:
IP
TCP
UDP
ports
connections
It doesn't necessarily need to understand:
GET /orders/123
or:
Host: api.example.com
or:
Authorization: Bearer ...
It can make decisions based on lower-level connection information.
________________________________________
L7 Load Balancer
An L7 load balancer can understand HTTP-level information:
GET /orders/123
Host: api.example.com
Cookie: ...
Now it can make decisions such as:
/api/orders/*    → Order service

/api/payments/*  → Payment service
So:
L4
 ↓
"I know this is TCP traffic."

L7
 ↓
"I know this is an HTTP request for /orders/123."
That's a massive conceptual difference.
________________________________________
5. The really important question
Suppose:
Client
   |
   | TCP connection #1
   |
   v
Load Balancer
   |
   | ???
   |
   v
Java Server
How does the LB communicate with the Java server?
This is where many developers have an incomplete mental model.
In many proxy-style architectures, you effectively have:
Client                    Load Balancer              Server
  │                              │                      │
  │──── TCP connection #1 ──────►│                      │
  │                              │──── TCP connection #2 ─────►
  │                              │                      │
  │                              │                      │
There can be two separate connections:
Connection #1:

Client
   │
   └──────────────► LB


Connection #2:

LB
   │
   └──────────────► Backend
The LB sits in the middle.
This is why a proxy/load balancer can perform operations such as:
TLS termination
connection management
backend selection
timeouts
health checks
header manipulation
depending on its architecture and layer.
________________________________________
6. Why two connections matter
Imagine:
Client → LB
has:
100ms latency
while:
LB → Java Server
has:
2ms latency
The LB is essentially acting as a middleman between two network relationships.
This means it can potentially terminate one connection and establish another.
For example:
Client
   |
   | HTTPS
   v
LB
   |
   | HTTP
   v
Java
That is:
Client ──TLS──► LB ──HTTP──► Java
The LB terminates TLS.
This is commonly called:
TLS termination
Why might we want that?
Because instead of every Java instance individually handling public TLS connections:
Internet
   |
   ├── TLS → Java A
   ├── TLS → Java B
   ├── TLS → Java C
   ├── TLS → Java D
   └── TLS → Java E
we can terminate TLS at the edge:
Internet
       |
       | HTTPS
       v
      LB
       |
       | HTTP
       v
   Private network
       |
   ┌───┼────┐
   ▼   ▼    ▼
  A    B    C
But this creates a security question:
Is HTTP between LB and Java acceptable?
Often not.
Instead:
Client
   |
   | HTTPS
   v
LB
   |
   | HTTPS
   v
Java
Now there are two TLS relationships.
TLS #1
Client ↔ LB

TLS #2
LB ↔ Java
That's called TLS re-encryption / TLS passthrough patterns, depending on exactly where termination occurs.
________________________________________
7. Now let's talk about the source IP
Suppose the real client is:
Client IP = 49.205.10.25
The request reaches:
Load Balancer
and the LB forwards it to:
10.0.1.25
What does the Java application see as the client IP?
Potentially:
10.0.1.100
—the LB's address.
Why?
Because from the backend's perspective, the immediate network peer may be the LB.
So:
Real client:

49.205.10.25

LB:

10.0.0.10

Java:

10.0.1.25
The Java server might see:
source = 10.0.0.10
instead of:
49.205.10.25
But applications often need the original client IP.
For example:
Rate limiting
Audit logs
Security
Fraud detection
Geo location
So the proxy can communicate the original address using mechanisms such as HTTP forwarding headers.
For HTTP, a common pattern is:
X-Forwarded-For: 49.205.10.25
Or standardized forwarding mechanisms can be used.
Your Spring application may then have:
remoteAddress
representing the proxy while a forwarded header carries the original client address.
Important production lesson
Never blindly trust:
X-Forwarded-For
from arbitrary internet clients.
Your infrastructure should define which proxies are trusted to set/append forwarding information.
Otherwise an attacker can potentially send:
X-Forwarded-For: 127.0.0.1
and fool poorly configured application logic.
________________________________________
8. NAT enters the story
Now we need to understand something called NAT — Network Address Translation.
Imagine:
Client:

49.205.10.25:52144
connects to:
LB:

203.0.113.10:443
At some point the LB needs to send traffic toward:
Backend:

10.0.1.25:8080
The network may perform address/port translation.
Conceptually:
49.205.10.25:52144
          |
          v
203.0.113.10:443
          |
          v
10.0.1.25:8080
The exact implementation differs depending on the load balancer architecture.
But the important mental model is:
Network addresses and ports can be translated as traffic crosses network boundaries.
________________________________________
9. Connection tracking
Now we're approaching something really interesting.
Suppose 100,000 clients are connected.
The LB needs to keep track of which connection belongs to which backend.
Conceptually, it may have state resembling:
Client connection
        ↓
10.20.30.40:52144
        ↓
Backend
        ↓
10.0.1.25:8080
Another:
10.20.30.41:61232
        ↓
10.0.1.26:8080
And so on.
Conceptually:
┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐
│ Connection Table                          │
├──────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ Client            Backend                 │
│ 10.20.30.40:52144 → 10.0.1.25:8080      │
│ 10.20.30.41:61232 → 10.0.1.26:8080      │
│ 10.20.30.42:44210 → 10.0.1.25:8080      │
└──────────────────────────────────────────┘
This is a major insight:
A "stateless" load-balancing architecture can still have connection state.
Why?
Because packets belonging to an established flow need to be associated correctly.
________________________________________
10. Why this state matters
Suppose the first packet goes:
Client → A
Then the next packet somehow goes:
Client → B
That's potentially disastrous.
The connection's traffic needs to remain associated with the right flow/backend depending on the LB design.
So a flow can conceptually be represented by something like:
Source IP
Source Port
Destination IP
Destination Port
Protocol
This is commonly called the 5-tuple:
(src IP,
 src port,
 dst IP,
 dst port,
 protocol)
For example:
49.205.10.25
52144
203.0.113.10
443
TCP
That combination identifies a TCP flow.
________________________________________
11. This gives us a new understanding of "load balancing"
The LB isn't necessarily choosing a backend for every packet independently.
It may choose a backend when a new flow/connection is established:
New flow
   ↓
Select backend
   ↓
Remember mapping
   ↓
Subsequent packets
   ↓
Same backend
Conceptually:
                    New connection
                          │
                          ▼
                 Backend selection
                          │
              ┌───────────┼───────────┐
              ▼           ▼           ▼
              A           B           C
                          │
                          ▼
                    Mapping stored
This distinction becomes crucial when we study:
•	connection persistence 
•	TCP 
•	HTTP keep-alive 
•	HTTP/2 
•	WebSockets 
•	sticky sessions 
