12. Now consider HTTP keep-alive
Suppose the client sends:
GET /users/1
GET /users/2
GET /users/3
It doesn't necessarily establish three TCP connections.
With connection reuse:
Client
   |
   | TCP connection
   |
   +── GET /users/1
   +── GET /users/2
   +── GET /users/3
   |
   v
LB
One TCP connection can carry multiple HTTP requests.
This means a load balancer may make a backend decision based on:
connection
rather than independently distributing:
request #1 → A
request #2 → B
request #3 → C
This is an extremely important distinction.
________________________________________
13. HTTP/2 makes it even more interesting
HTTP/1.1:
TCP connection
   |
   ├── Request 1
   ├── Request 2
   └── Request 3
HTTP/2 introduces multiplexing.
Conceptually:
One TCP connection
        |
        ├──── Stream 1 → /orders
        ├──── Stream 2 → /users
        ├──── Stream 3 → /payments
        └──── Stream 4 → /products
So now:
ONE TCP CONNECTION
        |
        +-- MANY concurrent HTTP streams
That has major consequences for load balancing.
Suppose:
Client
   |
   | HTTP/2 connection
   v
LB
   |
   v
Backend A
You don't necessarily get:
Stream 1 → A
Stream 2 → B
Stream 3 → C
just because the HTTP requests are separate.
Depending on architecture, the connection-level mapping can strongly influence where those streams go.
This is one reason connection-level balancing and request-level balancing are not the same problem.
We'll explore this much deeper later.
