1. First: what is a packet?
Suppose the client sends:
GET /orders/123
At the application level, you think:
HTTP request
But the network doesn't send "an HTTP request" as one magical object.
It gets encapsulated.
Conceptually:
┌───────────────────────────────────┐
│ Ethernet Frame                    │
│  ┌─────────────────────────────┐  │
│  │ IP Packet                   │  │
│  │  ┌───────────────────────┐  │  │
│  │  │ TCP Segment           │  │  │
│  │  │  ┌─────────────────┐  │  │  │
│  │  │  │ HTTP data       │  │  │  │
│  │  │  └─────────────────┘  │  │  │
│  │  └───────────────────────┘  │  │
│  └─────────────────────────────┘  │
└───────────────────────────────────┘
This is encapsulation.
Each layer adds its own information.
For example:
Ethernet
Source MAC
Destination MAC
IP
Source IP
Destination IP
TCP
Source port
Destination port
Sequence number
Acknowledgment number
Flags
Window
HTTP
GET /orders/123
This layering is important because an L4 load balancer doesn't need to understand all the way up to HTTP.
It can operate using:
IP + TCP
________________________________________
2. Packet arrives at the NIC
Imagine:
Client:

49.205.10.25:52144
connects to:
Load Balancer:

203.0.113.10:443
The physical/network interface receives an Ethernet frame.
Conceptually:
Internet
   │
   ▼
┌──────────┐
│   NIC    │
└────┬─────┘
     │
     ▼
Kernel networking
The NIC is not your application.
It's hardware.
The OS kernel then processes the incoming network traffic.
________________________________________
3. Kernel networking stack
Very simplified:
NIC
 ↓
Driver
 ↓
Network stack
 ↓
Ethernet processing
 ↓
IP processing
 ↓
TCP processing
 ↓
Socket
Eventually, the kernel determines:
"This packet belongs to this TCP connection."
That requires maintaining TCP state.
________________________________________
4. TCP isn't stateless
This is crucial.
Suppose:
Client → LB
has:
Source IP      = 49.205.10.25
Source port    = 52144
Destination IP = 203.0.113.10
Dest port      = 443
Protocol       = TCP
The connection isn't simply:
OPEN
TCP maintains state such as:
Sequence numbers
Acknowledgment numbers
Receive window
Send window
Connection state
Retransmission information
Timers
Conceptually:
TCP Connection
┌──────────────────────────────┐
│ src IP                       │
│ src port                     │
│ dst IP                       │
│ dst port                     │
│ state                        │
│ sequence state               │
│ acknowledgment state         │
│ window information           │
│ timers                       │
└──────────────────────────────┘
So when we said earlier:
"The load balancer has connection state."
This is what we're getting at.
________________________________________
5. TCP's 3-way handshake
Let's go deeper.
Client wants to establish:
Client → LB
Client sends:
SYN
Conceptually:
Client                        LB
  │                            │
  │ -------- SYN ------------> │
  │                            │
The SYN contains an initial sequence number.
For example:
ISN = 1000
The LB responds:
SYN + ACK
Conceptually:
Client                        LB
  │                            │
  │ -------- SYN ------------> │
  │                            │
  │ <------ SYN + ACK -------- │
  │                            │
Then:
ACK
Client                        LB
  │                            │
  │ -------- ACK ------------> │
  │                            │
Connection established.
Client ◄══════════════════════► LB
            TCP connection
________________________________________
6. Why sequence numbers matter
Imagine a packet gets lost.
Packet 1
Packet 2
Packet 3
Maybe packet 2 disappears.
The receiver can detect missing data through sequence/acknowledgment mechanisms.
TCP can then retransmit.
So TCP isn't:
send(bytes)
It's more like:
send bytes
↓
assign sequence numbers
↓
transmit
↓
receive acknowledgments
↓
detect loss
↓
retransmit
↓
control congestion
That means the LB isn't simply forwarding meaningless blobs.
It may be participating in or observing a stateful transport protocol.
________________________________________
7. Now the load balancer has a major architectural choice
Suppose:
Client
   │
   ▼
LB
   │
   ▼
Backend A
How does traffic move?
There are several fundamentally different approaches.
One common architecture is proxying:
Client
   │
   │ TCP connection A
   ▼
LB
   │
   │ TCP connection B
   ▼
Backend
The LB terminates the client-side TCP connection and creates another TCP connection toward the backend.
Conceptually:
                Load Balancer

       TCP #1             TCP #2
Client ══════════► LB ══════════► Backend
The LB is now actively participating in both connections.
________________________________________
8. Another possibility: packet forwarding
A high-performance L4 load balancer may instead forward/transform packets without behaving like a traditional full proxy at the application level.
Conceptually:
Client
   │
   │ packet
   ▼
LB
   │
   │ transformed/forwarded packet
   ▼
Backend
Now the LB can operate much closer to the packet-processing layer.
This distinction matters tremendously for performance.
A traditional proxy might need:
packet
 ↓
kernel
 ↓
socket
 ↓
user space
 ↓
LB logic
 ↓
socket
 ↓
kernel
 ↓
NIC
while specialized architectures can reduce expensive transitions.
________________________________________
9. User space vs kernel space
This is one of the most important concepts for understanding high-performance networking.
Normally:
Application
    │
    │ system call
    ▼
Kernel
    │
    ▼
NIC
The kernel controls networking.
For example:
Java application
      |
      | read()
      v
Linux kernel
      |
      v
Network stack
      |
      v
NIC
Why does the kernel do this?
Because it manages:
process isolation
memory protection
networking
files
sockets
TCP/IP
hardware
But crossing between user space and kernel space isn't free.
At massive packet rates, overhead matters.
________________________________________
10. Why normal proxying can become expensive
Imagine:
1,000,000 packets/sec
A traditional path could involve:
NIC
 ↓
Kernel
 ↓
Socket buffer
 ↓
User-space process
 ↓
LB logic
 ↓
Kernel
 ↓
NIC
There can be:
system calls
memory copies
context transitions
locking
cache misses
At enough scale, these become significant.
That's why high-performance networking systems use increasingly sophisticated techniques.
________________________________________
11. Event-driven networking
Here's the key idea.
Imagine 100,000 connections.
A naïve server might think:
Connection 1 → Thread 1
Connection 2 → Thread 2
Connection 3 → Thread 3
...
Connection 100000 → Thread 100000
This is terrible.
Threads consume memory.
Threads require scheduling.
Threads cause context switches.
And most connections are idle most of the time.
For example:
100,000 connections
but maybe only:
500 are currently receiving data
Why waste resources keeping 100,000 active threads?
Instead:
Use a small number of threads to monitor a large number of connections.
That's the fundamental idea behind event-driven I/O.
________________________________________
12. Enter epoll
On Linux, a central mechanism is:
epoll
Conceptually:
                Event Loop
                    │
                    ▼
                 epoll
                    │
        ┌───────────┼───────────┐
        ▼           ▼           ▼
     Socket 1    Socket 2    Socket 3
        │           │           │
       idle        data        idle
Instead of:
Thread 1 waits for Socket 1
Thread 2 waits for Socket 2
Thread 3 waits for Socket 3
we can have:
One event loop
       |
       +── monitor thousands of sockets
When data arrives:
Socket 2 → readable
the OS reports:
"Socket 2 is ready."
Then the event loop processes it.
________________________________________
13. The fundamental difference
Blocking model:
Thread
   |
   └── wait for socket
         ...
         ...
      data arrives
         |
         ▼
      process
Event-driven:
             Event Loop
                 |
        ┌────────┼────────┐
        ▼        ▼        ▼
      sock1    sock2    sock3
                 |
              readable
                 |
                 ▼
              process
This is why systems such as:
Netty
Nginx
HAProxy
Envoy
can handle enormous numbers of concurrent connections without creating one heavyweight thread per connection.
________________________________________
14. Now connect this to your Java knowledge
You've probably seen:
ExecutorService
and:
ThreadPoolExecutor
A traditional server model could look conceptually like:
accept()
   ↓
thread pool
   ↓
worker
   ↓
read request
   ↓
process
Netty is much more event-driven.
Conceptually:
                  EventLoopGroup
                        |
          ┌─────────────┼─────────────┐
          ▼             ▼             ▼
      EventLoop 1   EventLoop 2   EventLoop 3
          |             |             |
       sockets        sockets       sockets
The important idea:
One event-loop thread can manage many channels/connections.
The exact architecture is more nuanced than "one thread = many connections," but that's the mental model we need initially.
________________________________________
15. What does an event loop actually do?
Conceptually:
while (running) {

    events = waitForIOEvents();

    for (event : events) {

        if (event.isReadable()) {
            readData();
        }

        if (event.isWritable()) {
            writeData();
        }
    }
}
The important operation is:
waitForIOEvents()
Instead of constantly doing:
is socket 1 ready?
is socket 2 ready?
is socket 3 ready?
...
the kernel can efficiently tell the application which registered sockets are ready.
________________________________________
16. Why this is perfect for networking
Network workloads often look like:
Connection
   |
   | idle
   | idle
   | idle
   |
   | packet arrives
   ▼
process
   |
   | idle
   | idle
You don't need a dedicated thread doing nothing during those idle periods.
Instead:
Event loop
   |
   +── Socket A idle
   +── Socket B idle
   +── Socket C READY ← process
   +── Socket D idle
This is a fundamental scalability technique.
________________________________________
17. But there's a catch
Suppose you're in an event loop:
EventLoop Thread
      |
      ▼
read request
      |
      ▼
process request
      |
      ▼
call database
      |
      |
      | 500ms
      |
      ▼
response
If the DB call blocks the event-loop thread:
EventLoop
   |
   └── BLOCKED
what happens to other connections?
Connection A → waiting
Connection B → waiting
Connection C → waiting
Connection D → waiting
This is why event-driven systems are extremely sensitive to blocking operations.
You may need:
Event Loop
     |
     +── I/O events
     |
     +── dispatch
           |
           ▼
      Worker pool
           |
           ▼
     blocking work
This is one of the reasons understanding your Java execution model matters when you're designing high-throughput systems.
________________________________________
